# Can economics explain suicide?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What did Hamermesh and Soss argue? Where is the edge of the rational-choice approach?"
next: {"title":"Why vote when your vote almost never matters?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/96-why-vote-when-your-vote-almost-never.cast"}

## A model of the years ahead
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - '1928'
      - '1932'
    values:
      - 18
      - 22.1
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      decimals: 1
    y_label: Suicides per 100,000
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 25
    box:
      x: 250
      'y': 160
      w: 500
      h: 400
    label_size: 26
sources:
    - id: luo2011
      title: Impact of business cycles on US suicide rates, 1928–2007
      authors: Feijun Luo, Curtis S. Florence, Myriam Quispe-Agnoli, Lijing Ouyang, Alexander E. Crosby
      year: 2011
      finding: The overall US suicide rate surged from 18.0 per 100,000 in 1928 to 22.1, the all-time high, in 1932; it generally rose in recessions and fell in expansions (American Journal of Public Health 101(6):1139–46)
      doi: 10.2105/AJPH.2010.300010
    - id: hs1974
      title: An Economic Theory of Suicide
      authors: Daniel S. Hamermesh and Neal M. Soss
      year: 1974
      finding: Suicide is modelled as occurring when the discounted value of expected remaining lifetime utility falls below a threshold that differs across people; predicts rates rising with age and falling with permanent income, and rising with unemployment (Journal of Political Economy 82(1):83–98)
      doi: 10.1086/260171

    text depr "US, Great Depression" cites ["luo2011"] x 660 y 600 font_size 30 hidden true
    text hs "Hamermesh & Soss, 1974" cites ["hs1974"] x 500 y 625 font_size 30 hidden true
    text becker "after Becker: crime, family" #8f887c x 860 y 580 font_size 24 hidden true
    arrow xax #3d3833 from.x 100 from.y 100 to.x 960 to.y 100 hidden true
    arrow yax #3d3833 from.x 100 from.y 100 to.x 100 to.y 720 hidden true
    text xlab "age" x 880 y 40 font_size 50 hidden true
    text ylab "value of years ahead" x 420 y 705 font_size 50 hidden true
    path curve smooth #2f6b8f points [[130,610],[330,530],[530,430],[730,320],[900,225]] style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    path thr dashed thick #b5482e points [[100,190],[940,190]] hidden true
    text thrlab "threshold" #b5482e x 240 y 145 font_size 48 hidden true
    text jl "job loss" #2f6b8f x 330 y 320 font_size 48 hidden true
    figure model aspect 4:3 box.h 475 box.w 740 box.x 20 box.y 145 members ["xax","yax","xlab","ylab","curve","thr","thrlab","jl"] hidden true
    text c1 "older ↑" x 850 y 470 font_size 38 hidden true
    text c2 "poorer ↑" x 850 y 390 font_size 38 hidden true
    text c3 "jobless ↑" x 850 y 310 font_size 38 hidden true

    card title "Can economics explain suicide?"

Can economics, the science of prices and budgets, say anything about suicide?
    draw axes

B: A note first: this lecture is about suicide. If that touches your own life, help lines are on screen, now and at the end.
    text note "Content note: suicide. Help: 116 123 (Norway, UK, Ireland) · 988 (US)" #3d3833 x 500 y 665 font_size 28

It sounds almost improper. But look at America in the Great Depression.
    draw depr

In 1928, eighteen pe (@draw bar_1@)ople in every hundred thousand died by suicide.

By 1932 it was (@draw bar_2@) twenty-two, the highest rate the country has recorded.

B: So something economic moved something deeply private.
    highlight bar_2

    erase axes bar_1 bar_2 depr note

In 1974 two economists, Daniel Hamermesh and  (@draw xax yax xlab ylab@)Neal Soss, tried to model it — after Becker had already tak (@draw becker@)en the same tools to crime and to the family.
    draw hs

Their idea: a (@draw curve@) person weighs the whole life still ahead, every future year, discounted, with the income it is likely to bring.

    erase becker

B: And when that value falls below a threshold, the model predicts suicide.
    draw thr thrlab

The threshold differs from person to person, so the model speaks about rates in a population, never about one individual.
    highlight thr

    quiz "As people age, fewer years remain. What does the model predict for suicide rates by age?" voice b
        intro: Then here is the test of the logic.
        right: Rise with age.
        wrong: Fewer years ahead means a smaller sum of future years: here, rise with age.
        + Rise with age
        * Fall with age
        * No pattern

Fewer years ahead means a smaller sum, so the curve sinks toward the line.
    highlight curve

    keep curve

B: And income? Higher lifetime income lifts the whole curve; losing a job pulls it down, so rates should rise with unemployment.
    move curve by [0,-80] duration 2.5

    draw jl

Three predictions, all checkable:  (@draw c1@)older, poore (@draw c2@)r, jobless  (@draw c3@)means higher rates.

    pause 0.5

## What the data say
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 10–19
      - 20–29
      - 30–39
      - 40–49
      - 50–59
      - 60–69
      - 70+
    series:
      - {name: Germany, values: [2.6, 7.1, 8.4, 10.7, 15.5, 16.1, 30.3]}
      - {name: UK, values: [2.9, 12.4, 13.4, 15.4, 13.3, 9.4, 8.2]}
    points: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 35
    y_label: Suicides per 100,000
    x_label: age
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 200
      w: 820
      h: 400
    label_size: 24
sources:
    - id: who2021
      title: Crude suicide rates (per 100 000 population) by age group, 2021 (indicator SDGSUICIDE)
      authors: World Health Organization, Global Health Observatory
      year: 2024
      finding: '2021, both sexes, per 100,000 by age 10–19 … 70+: Germany 2.6, 7.1, 8.4, 10.7, 15.5, 16.1, 30.3; United Kingdom 2.9, 12.4, 13.4, 15.4, 13.3, 9.4, 8.2'
      url: https://ghoapi.azureedge.net/api/SDGSUICIDE
    - id: chang2013
      title: 'Impact of 2008 global economic crisis on suicide: time trend study in 54 countries'
      authors: Shu-Sen Chang, David Stuckler, Paul Yip, David Gunnell
      year: 2013
      finding: An estimated 4,884 (95% CI 3,907–5,860) excess suicides in 2009 compared with pre-crisis trends; increases mainly in men; rises larger where unemployment rose more (BMJ 347:f5239)
      doi: 10.1136/bmj.f5239
    - id: case2015
      title: Rising morbidity and mortality in midlife among white non-Hispanic Americans in the 21st century
      authors: Anne Case and Angus Deaton
      year: 2015
      finding: All-cause mortality of middle-aged white non-Hispanic Americans rose 1999–2013, largely from drug and alcohol poisonings, suicide and chronic liver disease; the least educated saw the largest increases (PNAS 112(49):15078–83)
      doi: 10.1073/pnas.1518393112
    - id: ruhm2019
      title: Drivers of the fatal drug epidemic
      authors: Christopher J. Ruhm
      year: 2019
      finding: County economic decline explains less than one-tenth of the 1999–2015 rise in drug and opioid death rates; the drug environment (availability and cost of drugs) matters more (Journal of Health Economics 64:25–42)
      doi: 10.1016/j.jhealeco.2019.01.001

    text src1 "2021, per 100,000" #8f887c cites ["who2021"] x 800 y 615 font_size 26 hidden true
    text big "≈ 4,900" #b5482e cites ["chang2013"] x 500 y 470 font_size 96 hidden true
    text bigsub "extra suicides, 2009 · 54 countries" x 500 y 370 font_size 34 hidden true
    text bigsub2 "mostly men · where jobs were lost" #2f6b8f x 500 y 290 font_size 34 hidden true
    box despair "deaths of despair\n(Case & Deaton)" cites ["case2015"] x 265 y 450 width 420 height 200 font_size 42 hidden true
    text dwords "drugs · alcohol · suicide" #8f887c x 265 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    box supply "or drug supply?\n(Ruhm)" cites ["ruhm2019"] x 735 y 450 width 420 height 200 font_size 42 hidden true
    text rwords "economy: < 1/10 of the rise" #8f887c x 735 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    text cont "contested" #b5482e x 500 y 220 font_size 56 hidden true

    card title "What the data say"

B: Age first. Here is Germany in 2021: suicide rates by age group.
    draw axes line_1 src1

It climbs steadily and is highest among the oldest, just as the model says.
    highlight line_1

B: Now Britain, same year. Where do you think its line goes after forty?
    ask "Draw Britain's line from age 40 on, then press Done." on line_2 from 3 store g
        right: Close: Britain's line turns down after midlife.
        wrong: Most people draw it rising like Germany's. Britain's turns down after midlife.

It peaks in midlife and falls among the old: the same theory fits one country and misses a near neighbour.
    highlight line_2

    erase axes line_1 line_2 src1

B: Unemployment does better. After the 2008 crash, a study across fifty-four countries counted abo (@draw big@)ut four thousand nine hundred more suicides in 2009 than earlier (@draw bigsub@) trends predicted, mainly in Europe and the Americas.

Mostly among men, and most where job losses were largest: a fa (@highlight big effect underline@)ll in expected income, as the model said.
    draw bigsub2

    erase big bigsub bigsub2

B: Then Anne Case and Angus Deaton found middle-aged white Americans dying younger after 1999, f (@draw dwords@)rom drugs, alcohol and suicide.
    draw despair

They called them deaths of despair, and they hit hardest those with the least education.
    highlight despair

B: But Christopher Ruhm found that local economic decline explains less than a tenth of the  (@draw rwords@)rise in drug deaths; he points to cheaper, stronger opioids.
    draw supply

So despair or supply? Both sides have se (@draw cont@)rious data, and economists still argue.

    pause 0.5

