sources:
    - id: whomdb
      title: Age-standardized death rate from self-inflicted injuries per 100,000 (United Kingdom), WHO Mortality Database
      authors: Our World in Data, from the WHO Mortality Database
      year: 2025
      finding: 'United Kingdom: 10.1 per 100,000 in 1963 (the peak), 6.7 in 1975 — a fall of about a third'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/suicide-rate-who-mdb
    - id: kreitman1976
      title: The coal gas story. United Kingdom suicide rates, 1960–71
      authors: Norman Kreitman
      year: 1976
      finding: Suicide by domestic gas fell in every age–sex group as the carbon-monoxide content of the gas fell; non-gas suicides rose in general, markedly in some groups, but not enough to offset it; a simple causal explanation was likely (British Journal of Preventive and Social Medicine 30(2):86–93)
      doi: 10.1136/jech.30.2.86
    - id: thomas2011
      title: 'Suicide epidemics: the impact of newly emerging methods on overall suicide rates — a time trends study'
      authors: Kyla Thomas, Shu-Sen Chang, David Gunnell
      year: 2011
      finding: Cites an approximately 30% reduction in suicides following coal-gas detoxification in England and Wales (BMC Public Health 11:314)
      doi: 10.1186/1471-2458-11-314
    - id: simon2001
      title: Characteristics of impulsive suicide attempts and attempters
      authors: Thomas R. Simon, Alan C. Swann, Kenneth E. Powell, Lloyd B. Potter, Marcie-jo Kresnow, Patrick W. O'Carroll
      year: 2001
      finding: Among 153 people aged 13–34 who made nearly lethal suicide attempts (Houston), 24% reported less than 5 minutes between the decision and the attempt (Suicide and Life-Threatening Behavior 32(1 Suppl):49–59)
      doi: 10.1521/suli.32.1.5.49.24212
    - id: cavanagh2003
      title: 'Psychological autopsy studies of suicide: a systematic review'
      authors: J. T. O. Cavanagh, A. J. Carson, M. Sharpe, S. M. Lawrie
      year: 2003
      finding: 'Median proportion of suicides with a mental disorder: 91% in case series, 90% in case-control studies (27% of controls) (Psychological Medicine 33(3):395–405)'
      doi: 10.1017/s0033291702006943
    - id: gunnell2017
      title: 'Prevention of suicide with regulations aimed at restricting access to highly hazardous pesticides: a systematic review of the international evidence'
      authors: David Gunnell, Duleeka Knipe, Shu-Sen Chang et al.
      year: 2017
      finding: National bans on commonly ingested pesticides were followed by fewer pesticide suicides in five of six countries and by falls in overall suicide in three (Lancet Global Health 5(10):e1026–37)
      doi: 10.1016/S2214-109X(17)30299-1
    - id: owens2002
      title: 'Fatal and non-fatal repetition of self-harm: systematic review'
      authors: David Owens, Judith Horrocks, Allan House
      year: 2002
      finding: Across 90 follow-up studies, after more than 9 years around 7% of people treated for self-harm had died by suicide (British Journal of Psychiatry 181:193–9)
      doi: 10.1192/bjp.181.3.193
    - id: chang2013
      title: 'Impact of 2008 global economic crisis on suicide: time trend study in 54 countries'
      authors: Shu-Sen Chang, David Stuckler, Paul Yip, David Gunnell
      year: 2013
      finding: About 4,900 excess suicides in 2009 against pre-crisis trends, larger where unemployment rose more (BMJ 347:f5239)
      doi: 10.1136/bmj.f5239

    text gascue "gas loses its carbon monoxide" #8f887c at.data [1970,4.2] font_size 28 hidden true
    text n63 "10.1 (1963)" at.data [1963,11] font_size 30 hidden true
    text n75 "6.7 (1975)" at.data [1976,5.6] font_size 30 hidden true
    text third "−1/3" #b5482e at.data [1971,9.4] cites ["whomdb","thomas2011"] font_size 48 hidden true
    text kre "Kreitman 1976: other methods up a little" #2f6b8f at.data [1968,1.8] cites ["kreitman1976"] font_size 28 hidden true
    text imp "24%: under 5 minutes" #b5482e at.data [1972,3] cites ["simon2001"] font_size 36 hidden true
    text mh "~90%: a mental disorder" #b5482e at.data [1972,3] cites ["cavanagh2003"] font_size 36 hidden true
    text hsure "How sure are we?" x 500 y 635 font_size 40 hidden true
    text s1 "Recessions raise suicide: mostly agreed" #5d7f4e cites ["chang2013"] x 500 y 560 font_size 36 hidden true
    text s2 "Removing a lethal means saves lives: mostly agreed" #5d7f4e cites ["kreitman1976","gunnell2017"] x 500 y 470 font_size 36 hidden true
    text s3 "Suicide as a rational choice: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 380 font_size 36 hidden true
    text edge "explains rates · not the person" x 500 y 590 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Where the model stops"

Evidence that fits the model is one thing. Here is a fact it struggles with.
    draw axes

B: Britain in  (@draw line_1@)the 1950s. Household gas was coal gas, full of carbon monoxide, and it was a common means of suicide.

Through the 1960s and early seventi (@draw gascue@)es the gas supply lost its carbon monoxide, and North Sea natural gas took over.

B: Draw what you think happened to the total suicide rate.
    ask "Draw the total suicide rate after 1963, then press Done." on line_2 from 13 store g
        right: Close: the total fell by about a third.
        wrong: Most people expect the total to hold steady. It fell by about a third.

    erase line_1

It fell by a third: from a (@draw n63@)bout ten per hundred thousand in 1963 t (@draw n75@)o under seven by 1 (@draw third@)975.

B: Norman Kreitman studied i (@draw kre@)t in 1976: gas suicides all but vanished, and other methods rose, but far too little to fill the gap.

A pure rational-choice model says a settled decision finds another way. Most people did not.
    highlight line_2

    erase gascue kre

B: Because many suicidal crises are short. In one study of near-fa (@draw imp@)tal attempts, a quarter decided less than five minutes before.

    erase imp

And in about nine in ten suicides studied  (@draw mh@)afterwards, the person had a mental disorder, which changes how the years ahead look from inside.

B: So the weighing the model describes is done by a mind that may not see the future clearly — and the crisis often passes.
    fade mh to 0.35

    erase mh

And most people do come through: in follow-up studies of people treated after self-harm, fewer than one in ten had died by suicide nine or more years later.
    text most "most people come through" #5d7f4e at.data [1972,3] cites ["owens2002"] font_size 40

    erase axes line_2 n63 n75 third mh most

    draw hsure

Still, the model earned its place: (@draw s1@) it said recessions would cost lives, (@draw s2@) and they do.

Mostly agreed on that; contested a (@draw s3@)s an account of the single choice.

    quiz "A country bans the most lethal pesticides, a common method of suicide there. What does the coal-gas evidence lead you to expect?" intro "Two questions to finish."
        right: Overall suicides fall: removing an easy, lethal means saves lives, and after several national pesticide bans the total fell.
        wrong: In Britain, few switched when the gas became safe: here, overall suicides fall.
        + Overall suicides fall
        * People switch; the total stays
        * Suicides rise

    quiz "Harbourtown's boatyard, its biggest employer, closes. What does Hamermesh and Soss's model predict for the town's suicide rate?"
        right: It rises: job loss lowers expected lifetime income, and the evidence on recessions agrees.
        wrong: Job loss lowers expected lifetime income: here, it rises.
        + It rises
        * It falls
        * It doesn't change

    erase s1 s2 s3 hsure

B: And if any of this touches your own life, these lines answer at any hour; anywhere else, your local emergency number does.
    text helph "If you need to talk, now:" #3d3833 x 500 y 440 font_size 34
    text help1 "Norway: Mental Helse 116 123" #3d3833 x 500 y 380 font_size 34
    text help2 "UK & Ireland: Samaritans 116 123" #3d3833 x 500 y 325 font_size 34
    text help3 "US: 988 · elsewhere: your local emergency number" #3d3833 x 500 y 270 font_size 34

    pause 0.4

Economics can explain the rates — when jobs vanish, more lives are lost — but not the person; that is the edge of its tools.
    draw edge

## Where next
